            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186064078,
              ":question/id": "115b3fb1-1041-532a-b5fa-4d8a53c9ab2a",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-450",
              ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\textrm{d}x},$ writing your final answer as a single function.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "First, let $u=1+2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+2x}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|1+2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C = \\dfrac 1 2\\ln K$ for another arbitrary constant $K > 0,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|1+2x|+C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|1+2x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(K\\cdot |1+2x|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(K|1+2x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\textrm{d}x  = \\dfrac 1 2 \\ln \\left(K |1+2x|\\right).\n$$"
